            "source_url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_1/The_Doctrine_of_the_Mean",
            "text": "It is said in the Book of Poetry, \"I regard with pleasure your brilliant virtue, making no great display of itself in sounds and appearances.\" The Master said, \"Among the appliances to transform the people, sound and appearances are but trivial influences. It is said in another ode, 'His Virtue is light as a hair.' Still, a hair will admit of comparison as to its size. 'The doings of the supreme Heaven have neither sound nor smell. 'That is perfect virtue.\"",
            "translator": "Legge",
            "year": 1885
          }
